WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
Blood moves fastest in your widest artery and slowest in your narrowest vessels.

2
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:11.020
How can narrower mean slower?

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.560
A single river channel does the opposite.

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.320
Where its cross-section shrinks, the same water has to speed up.

5
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:22.943
Start with what makes blood flow along a vessel.

6
00:00:24.243 --> 00:00:28.523
In a level tube, fluid flows only from higher pressure to lower pressure.

7
00:00:29.883 --> 00:00:34.263
Equal pressure at both ends drives no flow, however high that pressure is.

8
00:00:35.283 --> 00:00:37.503
How much flows depends on two things.

9
00:00:38.703 --> 00:00:41.103
One is the pressure difference between the ends.

10
00:00:42.263 --> 00:00:44.343
The other is the resistance in the way.

11
00:00:45.763 --> 00:00:49.063
Flow equals the pressure difference divided by the resistance.

12
00:00:50.217 --> 00:00:53.957
Now lay out the whole circulation as one branching tree.

13
00:00:55.237 --> 00:00:59.137
The aorta splits into arteries, then arterioles, then capillaries.

14
00:01:00.457 --> 00:01:04.177
The capillaries gather again into veins that return to the heart.

15
00:01:05.217 --> 00:01:07.317
Above the tree, follow the pressure.

16
00:01:08.597 --> 00:01:12.017
It falls all the way from the aorta to the heart's right atrium.

17
00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:15.657
It falls most steeply across the arterioles.

18
00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:18.967
Arterioles have walls of smooth muscle.

19
00:01:20.227 --> 00:01:22.947
When the muscle tightens, the arteriole narrows.

20
00:01:24.007 --> 00:01:28.787
In an ideal tube, resistance rises with the fourth power of a falling radius.

21
00:01:30.007 --> 00:01:31.907
A small squeeze makes a large change.

22
00:01:32.917 --> 00:01:34.877
So arterioles act like taps.

23
00:01:36.437 --> 00:01:39.237
Nerves, hormones and local signals set their tone.

24
00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:44.277
Tightening one organ's arterioles cuts that organ's share of the blood.

25
00:01:45.317 --> 00:01:48.797
But a capillary is narrower than a typical arteriole.

26
00:01:49.957 --> 00:01:51.917
On its own, it can resist more.

27
00:01:53.137 --> 00:01:55.137
So which level sets most of the resistance?

28
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.680
The arterioles. Most capillaries are not lined up in a chain.

29
00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.420
They lie side by side, in huge numbers.

30
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:15.560
Side by side, paths share the flow, so together they resist less.

31
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.090
Now return to speed.

32
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:24.010
In this simplified tree at steady state, no blood bypasses the capillaries.

33
00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.350
So the same flow crosses every level.

34
00:02:29.633 --> 00:02:33.913
Speed is that flow divided by the total cross-section at each level.

35
00:02:35.213 --> 00:02:36.453
Each capillary is tiny.

36
00:02:37.693 --> 00:02:40.773
But together their cross-section far exceeds the aorta's.

37
00:02:41.817 --> 00:02:46.037
Spread the same flow over that huge area, and it has to move slowly.

38
00:02:47.297 --> 00:02:51.577
That is why blood creeps through the capillaries, the narrowest vessels of all.

39
00:02:52.617 --> 00:02:55.037
So narrow does not, by itself, mean slow.

40
00:02:56.357 --> 00:02:59.957
A single narrow tube carrying the whole flow would speed blood up.

41
00:03:01.137 --> 00:03:03.677
What matters is the total area at each level.

42
00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:08.357
That slow passage gives blood time to trade with the tissues.

43
00:03:09.657 --> 00:03:14.577
And fluid crossing the capillary wall obeys the same rule: a pressure difference

44
00:03:14.577 --> 00:03:15.537
over a resistance.
